The '''Baliscan Revolution''' ({{lang-bl|Revolución baliscana}}), also known as the '''''Comuncazo''''' or the '''Second''' '''Baliscan Civil War''' ({{Langx|bl|Segunda Guerra Civil Baliscana}}) was a [[w:Social revolution|social revolution]] and {{W|mass movement}} that spread across Balisca in the late 1910s. It followed years of voluntary {{W|communization}}, increasing {{W|postnationalism|non-nationalism}} as a result of shifting demographic across the country and increasing radicalization of {{W|decolonization|anticolonial}} and {{W|anti-imperialism|anti-imperialist}} movements that supported the idea of {{W|proletarian internationalism}}. The Revolution reached a precipice following the [[Storming of the Baliscan Cabildo|''Castejónazo'']] insurrection and the subsequent events of the [[Tragic Week]]. 

The revolution resulted in the abolition of the Baliscan republican system and the widespread implementation of [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchist]] and more specifically [[w:libertarian socialism|libertarian socialist]] principles throughout various portions of society, including the {{W|free migration|abolition of internal borders}}, {{W|secularization}}, and {{W|democratic socialism}}. Much of the [[economy of Balisca]] was put under {{W|worker control}}, while {{W|social welfare}} was significantly expanded through the {{W|redistribution of wealth|redistribution of income and wealth}}. Worker committees gained control of their factories, [[w:agriculture|agrarian]] areas became collectivized and run as libertarian socialist {{W|agricultural commune}}s.

The mass empowerment and embrace of various groups in this bottom-up system has been described as a "{{W|people's commune}}" or an "[[w:anarchism|anarchist]] {{W|republic}}", one based on the theories of {{W|anarcho-communism}} and built on a network of [[trade unions in Balisca|trade union]]s, {{W|factory committee}}s and [[w:Popular assembly|popular assemblies]]. The assemblies were used by the local citizens as a form of {{W|referendum}}s, where decisions were made through {{W|direct democracy}}, and became the basis for [[w:Civil law (common law)|civil law]]. The vast majority of Balisca's political decisions were made independently of {{W|Central Authority}}, through a system of {{W|libertarian municipalism|radical libertarian}} {{W|self-governance}} at the [[communes of Balisca|communal]] and [[cantons of Balisca|cantonal]] levels. Networks of "[[W:Soviet democracy|free councils]]" acted as institutions of {{W|participatory democracy}}, where issues would be discussed and dealt with directly.

The revolution had far reaching effects in Balisca's economic structure with the expanded power of workers and their unions, ultimately leading to the development of Balisca's current form of {{W|market socialism}}. Socioeconomic policy was also heavily influenced by the [[W:Futurism|futurist]] movements that had been growing in popularity prior to the revolution, eventually coalescing into [[Baliscan Accelerationism|accelerationist]] ideology. In addition, the Revolution hastened the process of {{W|indigenous decolonization}} which had been started by the [[1883 Confederal Convention]]. Additionally, the Revolution has significant international repercussions, especially regarding Baliscan relations with emerging leftist movements across the world. The revolution served as the precursor to similar social movements in [[Lurando-Abyala]] and other regions.

== Background ==

=== Legacy of the Cantonal Revolution ===

{{main|Cantonal Revolution|1883 Confederal Convention}}

The Baliscan Revolution emerged from a revolutionary tradition stretching back to the [[Cantonal Revolution]] of 1873–1880. The Cantonal Revolution had been a failed uprising against the centralization of power under President [[Michelangelo Valentino]] during the [[Anyos Federales|Federal Years]]. Though militarily defeated, the Cantonal Revolution established crucial precedents: it demonstrated that a broad coalition of workers, indigenous peoples, and regional movements could mount a serious challenge to federal authority.

The suppression of the Cantonal Revolution produced two lasting legacies. First, the limited reforms of 1882–1885—including [[W:Universal male suffrage|universal male suffrage]], limited [[W:Right to unionize|labor rights]], and the replacement of provinces with [[cantons of Balisca|cantons]]—created political openings that had not previously existed. Second, and more significantly, the [[1883 Confederal Convention]] produced a shadow constitution that embodied the Cantonalist vision: genuine cantonal autonomy, [[W:Direct democracy|direct democracy]] through popular assemblies, indigenous self-governance, full labor rights, and a federal government strictly limited to foreign affairs, defense, and inter-cantonal coordination.

Published by the anarchist periodical ''[[La Solidaridad]]'' in 1905, the shadow constitution circulated secretly among opposition networks for decades. It provided an ideological foundation for the 1918–1922 revolution, with many revolutionaries citing the 1883 document as the legitimate basis for the post-revolutionary order.

=== Baliscan Gilded Age ===

{{main|Baliscan Gilded Age}}

Following the Cantonal Revolution, Balisca entered a period of rapid industrialization and economic growth known as the [[Baliscan Gilded Age]] (approximately 1880–1914). The federal government under President [[Josèp Bernat]] and his successors pursued aggressively pro-business policies, providing subsidies to railroads, suppressing labor organizing, and facilitating the extraction of resources from the interior. The economy grew dramatically, but the benefits concentrated in the hands of a small elite of industrialists, landowners, and foreign investors.

The Gilded Age produced stark inequality. By 1910, the top 5% of the population controlled approximately 70% of national wealth. The [[Gini coefficient]] reached an estimated 0.72—among the highest in the world. Working conditions in factories, mines, and agricultural plantations were often brutal, with long hours, dangerous conditions, and poverty wages. The [[History of the labor movement in Balisca|labor movement]], though constrained by the limitations of the 1884-85 reforms, grew steadily, organizing strikes and building networks of [[W:Mutual aid|mutual aid]].

Indigenous communities faced accelerating encroachment on traditional territories as industrialists sought more resources to meet increasing resource demands for manufacturing. The federal government accelerated the distribution of indigenous lands to political allies and foreign investors, "losing" treaties and opening territories under the [[W:Homestead principle|homestead principle]]. Decades of court battles produced limited success, prompting indigenous communities to seek allies through militarism, and among labor and political movements.

=== Darasian influence and Maana Kadar ===
{{Main|Marxist-Jianism|Kadarism}}

The [[Darasian Socialist Revolution]] of 1904–1905 provided both inspiration and theoretical influence. Darasian [[Marxism–Jianism|Jianist socialism]] reached Balisca through translated texts, returning seamen, and political exiles. The [[Maana Kadar]] synthesis—developing Burawi socialist theory in dialogue with Darasian experiences—circulated through Kaftian-Baliscan intellectual and religious networks.

Kadar's visit to Daras in 1906 established connections between Darasian and [[Socialism in Kaftia|Kaftian socialist movements]]. His correspondence with Darasian leader [[Chen Lixian]]—published as ''[[Letters Between Continents]]'' in 1915—influenced a generation of Baliscan socialists and introduced Jianist concepts to Baliscan political discourse. However, Baliscan revolutionaries, building on the [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchist tradition]] inherited from the [[Cantonal Revolution]], adapted Jianist principles to a specifically anti-authoritarian framework.

=== Indigenous political development ===

Building on the legacy of the [[Maroon Wars]] and the [[Maroon Confederacy]], revolutionary indigenous political thought developed through the [[Indigenous Cultural Renaissance]] of the 1890s–1910s. This movement synthesized traditional governance practices with anti-colonial theory and the emerging socialist discourse. Figures like [[Ñanderú Kuarahy]] articulated indigenous [[W:Communalism|communalism]] that prefigured and influenced the revolutionary synthesis.

The [[Pindorama Movement]] of the 1910s demanded indigenous sovereignty, communal land rights, and the end of colonial extraction arrangements. The movement's 1915 '''Declaration of Pindorama''' asserted the right of indigenous nations to self-governance within a confederal framework—a position that anticipated the post-revolutionary arrangements.

=== Anarchist movement and the FAT-CAT ===

{{main|Anarchism in Balisca}}

The [[anarchism in Balisca|anarchist movement]] had grown substantially since the Cantonal Revolution, mostly organizing clandestinely. The [[Federación Anárquica del Trabajo]] - [[Confederación Autónoma del Trabajo]] (FAT-CAT), both established in the 1880s, provided organizational infrastructure for anarchist activity. Anarchist migrants from [[Andalus]], [[Hersatia]], and [[Volisania]] established influential circles, while indigenous activists contributed traditions of decentralized governance.

The ''[[Cosmocazo]]'' tradition—the "cosmic transformation" ideology that had animated the Cantonal Revolution—was reinterpreted by early 20th-century anarchists as a specifically social revolutionary program. The synthesis of anarchism with indigenous communalism and Jianist socialism produced a distinctive Baliscan revolutionary ideology that rejected both liberalism and authoritarian socialism.

=== Communization and dual power ===

Beginning around 1910, a process of voluntary [[W:Communization|communization]] had taken route through Baliscan society in face of the [[W:State capture|government's capture]] by corporate interests and the absence of state assistance. This shift was particularly visible within rural Balisca, where the rural poor consolidated with their neighbors and engaged in [[W:Collectivized agriculture|collectivized agriculture]] as a way of staying afloat. These communities were being squeezed by landlords in the cities with high taxes. In more isolated areas, these communities seized unused lands and established communal agriculture. Indigenous communities reasserted traditional governance over contested territories. Urban neighborhoods had witnessed the spread mutual aid networks (primarily in the form of communal kitchens, [[W:Food pantry|food pantries]], and [[W:Self-managed social center|autonomous social centers]]), cooperative housing, and [[W:Popular assembly|popular assemblies]].

These developments occurred largely outside state channels, creating a [[W:Dual power|dual power]] situation in which revolutionary institutions paralleled and increasingly supplanted federal authority. By 1917, large portions of [[rural Balisca]] and significant urban districts operated under de facto cooperative governance, with federal officials either ejected or ignored.
== Course of the revolution ==

=== Storming of the Baliscan Cabildo ===
{{main|Storming of the Baliscan Cabildo}}

The revolution's opening event occurred on 21 April 1918, when approximately 500 rebelling soldiers and revolutionaries stormed and occupied the [[Baliscan Cabildo]] in [[Castejón]]. The ''Castejónazo'' (from "Castejón" and the ''-azo'' suffix of violent augmentation) was a reaction to increasingly violent repression by the administration of President [[Máximo Burjassot]], whose government had escalated force against labor organizing, indigenous movements, and political dissent in an attempt to quell growing unrest and reassure the oligarchic elite.

The storming and subsequent siege of the Cabildo was a significant development, marking the collapse of military loyalty to the Burjassot administration and the beginning of the revolutionary phase proper. The coup demonstrated that the state's monopoly on force had shattered; significant portions of the military had been radicalized by the labor and anarchist movements that had been organizing within the ranks.

=== Tragic Week ===
{{main|Tragic Week (Balisca)}}

[[File:Leaving the danger zone (5738314802).jpg|thumb|Civilians fleeing the danger zone in [[Llanito]] 26 April 1919]]
The Tragic Week ({{lang|bl|La Semana Trájika}}), alternatively known as the Seven Days of Lead ({{lang|bl|Sete Dias de Plomo}}), was a series of violent reprisals and counter-reprisals during the autumn of 1919. The fighting pitted the Baliscan military against anarchists, freemasons, socialists, and revolutionaries across Castejón and other major cities.

For uninvolved citizens, the seven days of violence, displacement, and uncertainty were horrific. While most fighting occurred between opposing armed factions—assaulting or defending the growing power of the workers' councils—artillery and rifle fire inflicted substantial losses among civilians and caused major damage to property in the capital's downtown. The Tragic Week marked the beginning of the second, more violent phase of the Baliscan Revolution, leading to a significant rise in [[W:Political violence|political violence]] nationwide.

=== 1919 Communist coup attempt ===
{{main|1919 Baliscan coup attempt}}

In June 1919, during elections for the [[1919 Confederal Convention]], tensions within the revolutionary coalition reached a breaking point. The [[Communist Party of Balisca]], skeptical of the abilities of the [[W:Lumpenproletariat|''subproletariado'']] around the [[W:Marxism|Marxist]] idea of the [[W:Dictatorship of the proletariat|dictatorship of the proletariat]] and the need for a [[W:vanguard party|vanguard party]] to guide the country on the path to communism, launched a coup attempt from 13 to 20 June 1919. The uprising intended to prevent the consolidation of anarchist influence in the Convention and establish a centralized [[Marxist–Jianist state]] under the leadership of the PCB as the [[W:Vanguardism|vanguard]].

The coup failed due to lack of popular support, internal fragmentation within the Communist Party, and decisive resistance by [[Autonomous Militia|Autonomous Militias]] loyal to the democratic revolutionary movement. In a brief but intense series of urban confrontations known as the [[May Days]], the communists declared a [[Provisional Central Committee]] as a rival governing authority and attempted to arrest or assassinate anarchist delegates. [[W:Anarcho-syndicalism|Anarcho-syndicalist]] unions—dominant in transport, dockyards, printing, and metallurgy—mobilized against the coup. Their mutual aid infrastructure, including free schools, food distribution networks, medical brigades, and printing cooperatives, had provided vital support during the social upheaval.

By the end of June, the last communist holdouts had surrendered or gone into hiding. The majority of rank-and-file rebels were spared; only senior party leaders and conspirators were tried for sedition and jailed. The coup attempt had a profound effect on revolutionary political culture, demonstrating the danger of vanguardist organization and party discipline imposed from outside. The event became a central argument for [[Basic Law: Political Parties, Organization and Representation|Article IV of the Basic Laws]], which abolished political parties entirely.

==== Spread of revolutionary forces ====
Following the defeat of the communist coup, revolutionary forces rapidly consolidated control across the [[Levante, Balisca|Baliscan Levante]]. The [[Autonomous Militia|People's Army]]—formed from defecting federal units, workers' militias, and indigenous allies—expanded outward from Castejón. [[Pindorama]] formally asserted indigenous sovereignty, allied with but autonomous from the revolutionary government. Indigenous forces played crucial roles throughout the revolution. The [[Aruanãyára]] militias under [[Ñanderú Kuarahy]] provided essential manpower and knowledge of interior terrain. The [[Anhangá Sovereignty Army]], which had moved to secured the Pindoramese highlands following the ''Castejónazo'', would negotiate the [[Charter of Yvyra'ara]] (1919) that assured indigenous self-governance. Indigenous communities would later contribute substantially to the constitutional development of the post-revolutionary system.

In rural Araucaria, particularly near the border with Asikyira, the ''Malakate Brigades''—would utilize the [[Lucumí Tarica|spiritual traditions]] of resistance of the [[Maroon Wars]] to sustain morale, and linked the revolution to anti-colonial traditions. [[Babatúndé Olowu-Kuti]], the musical theorist and composer, served as a propagandist and organizer, producing the revolutionary songs that became ''[[Balisca-nova]]''.

Federal loyalist forces retreated to southern [[Estuaria]] ([[Canyars]]) where they were able to retrieve supplies and limited aid from Sar and [[Trás-as-Montanyas]], where they received support from [[Volisania]] and [[Potocsí]].

=== Foreign intervention ===
{{Main|Foreign intervention in the Baliscan Revolution|Republic of Meridiana}}

The revolution attracted immediate foreign intervention. [[Sanrobonia]] and [[Potocsí]], fearing the spread of revolutionary sentiment to their own territories and seeking to protect substantial investments in Balisca, deployed an expeditionary force of approximately 45,000 troops in November 1918. [[Potocsí]], aligned with Sanrobonia and hostile to indigenous sovereignty movements, contributed 18,000 troops. Both powers provided equipment, naval support, and advisory assistance to republican forces. On the other side, the [[Daras|Darasian Socialist Republic]] provided [[Darasian involvement in the Baliscan Revolution|military aid]], equipment, and advisory support to the revolutionary forces. This assistance, though limited by the [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]] blockade of Daras, proved symbolically and practically significant. The [[Baliscan-Darasian Friendship Treaty]] of 1919 formalized the relationship.
[[File:Flag of the Meridian Republic.svg|thumb|Flag of the [[Republic of Meridiana]], a [[Potocsí|Potocsino]] proxy (1919-1922)]]
The [[Sanrobonia|Sanrobonian]] government allowed transit of a substantial amount materials to Nationalist forces and provided diplomatic support in international forums. Following the Nationalist withdrawal to their bases of support in [[Canyars]], they were able retrieve supplies and other forms of material from Potocsino and Sanrobonian governments that hoped to undermine the Baliscan revolutionaries. On 20 January 1919, the Potocsino military announced its intention to [[Potocsino occupation of Canyars|annex the territory of Canyars]], sparking outrage amongst many Nationalist leaders and local communities. While vocally opposing the annexation, Nationalist forces assisted Potocsino forces in [[1919 massacres in Canyars|violently suppressing the local protests]], in fear of them being revolutionary-aligned. Discussions between the Nationalists and the Potocsino occupation forces would result in the formation of the [[Republic of Meridiana]], a [[W:Puppet state|puppet]]/[[W:Buffer state|buffer state]] consisting of the Potocsino-occupied territories in Southern Balisca.

As the revolution progressed, and especially after the 1919 massacres in Estuaria, the Nationalists increasingly fell under the influence of it's foreign supporters, who considered the Nationalists as "servile satellites" with little independence. This gave the Nationalist movement a reputation as "undignified puppets", and traitors, largely discrediting the anti-revolutionaries amongst their supporters, while the revolutionaries increasingly appeared more dignified and patriotic, driving former nationalist supporters into joining with the revolutionaries instead. The Potocsino intervention helped to bolster the revolutionary, as they also successfully used this to attack the Nationalists.

== International reactions ==

=== Daras ===
{{Main|Balisca-Daras relations}}

The [[Daras|Darasian Socialist Republic]]—established through its own revolution of 1904–1905—viewed the Baliscan Revolution with a mixture of solidarity and theoretical concern. The Darasian model of "Heavenly Emperor" socialism, synthesizing imperial symbolism with socialist economics, differed substantially from the Baliscan anti-statist path. Darasian observers debated whether the Baliscan approach represented correct application of [[Marxism–Jianism|Jianist]] principles or dangerous deviation. Despite theoretical tensions, Daras provided crucial support. The [[Baliscan-Darasian Friendship Treaty]] (1919) formalized military aid, technical assistance, and trade arrangements. Darasian advisors contributed to economic planning and military organization. The [[Maana Kadar]] visit of 1922—following the Burawi theorist's earlier 1906 visit to Daras—established connections that would shape socialist thought across [[Kaftia]] and [[Abyala]].

=== Potocsí ===

{{main|Camboriú War|Potocsí–Balisca relations}}

[[Potocsí]] opposed the revolution from the outset, providing troops and material support to the Nationalist forces. The [[Potocsino Expedition]] of 18,000 troops invaded the territory of Estuaria and Pindorama in the summer of 1919, seizing significant territory in [[Canyars]], which they intended to annex. Nationalist and local Estuarian opposition led to the abandonment of this plan-the buffer state of [[Republic of Meridiana|Meridiana]], a Potocsino puppet state in all but name, was established in its place. The puppet state would survive until it's invasion and dissolution by the [[Confederal Congress]] in 1924.

Potocsí's hostility towards the revolution stemmed from a variety of positions, including longstanding [[anti-Baliscan sentiment]], [[W:Anti-socialism|anti-socialist]], [[W:Anti-communism|anti-communist]], and [[W:Proto-fascism|proto-fascist]] ideology in Potocsino politics, and concern that the [[W:Red scare|revolution would inspire]] similar leftist or indigenous movements within Potocsian territory. Relations remained hostile throughout the subsequent decades, with further conflicts including the [[Baliscan-Potocsino War]] of 1924 (in which Balisca recaptured all lost Estuarian territory), and culminating in the [[Camboriú War]] of 1976–1979.

=== Sanrobonia ===

{{main|Balisca-Sanrobonia relations|}}

[[Sanrobonia]] maintained formal neutrality while providing tacit support to interventionist forces. Sanrobonian ports served as transit points for anti-revolutionary supplies, and Sanrobonian diplomatic personnel advocated for the republican cause in international forums. Following the revolution's conclusion, Sanrobonia became a destination for counter-revolutionary exiles and went on to adopt a hostile diplomatic posture toward the new Baliscan regime. As the Baliscan revolutionary regime consolidated control in the Northern Triangle these tensions would develop into the [[Abyalan Cold War]].

== Legacy ==

=== Monuments and symbols ===
[[File:Los_revolucionarios_en_la_porta.jpg|thumb|335x335px|''Revolutionaries at the Gate'' (1928) by Edward Moran. Oil on canvas. [[Museum of the Metropolitan City of Castejón]].]]Revolutionary monuments and symbols permeate Baliscan public space. The [[Los Revolucionarios|''Los Revolucionarios'']] statue complex on [[tba Island]]<nowiki/>in [[Salto Bay]] welcomes ships through the [[Guadalquivir]]. The statue, of two figures with a hammer and a sickle raised over their heads, represents the union of the [[W:Working class|working class]] (hammer) and [[W:Peasantry|peasantry]] (sickle) in socialist ideology, and recognizes the necessity of both for successful [[W:Social revolution|social revolution]]. The monument is one of the prime examples of Baliscan socialist realism, embodying industrialization, solidarity, and the "eternal union" of agriculture and industry.

The [[W:Red and black flag|Red and Black flag]]—combining the socialist red with the anarchist black—remains the symbol of revolutionary Balisca, flown alongside the confederal tricolor. The phrase "[[W:No gods, no masters|No gods, no masters]]" (''Ni dioses, ni amos'') remains a popular slogan. [[Canto revolucionario|Revolutionary songs]], particularly the works of [[Maria da Silva Campos]] and [[Babatúndé Olowu-Kuti]], continue to be performed and taught across Balisca, and have been adapted into other languages across Adonia.

=== Historical assessment ===

Historical assessment of the revolution remains overwhelmingly positive within Balisca, where it is understood as the founding moment of contemporary society. International scholarship debates the revolution's significance relative to the [[Darasian Socialist Revolution|Darasian]] and [[Burawi Domestic War|Burawi]] revolutions, with some scholars arguing that the Baliscan example demonstrates the possibility of non-authoritarian (state) socialism while others contend that Balisca's exceptional circumstances limit the model's applicability elsewhere.

The revolution's treatment of indigenous sovereignty—the [[Charter of Yvyra'ara]] and subsequent arrangements—has received particular scholarly attention as a relatively successful example of post-revolutionary decolonization, though critics note ongoing tensions and incomplete implementation.

